<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Balliol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1283–<a href="/years/1364">1364</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Balliol was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> Balliol and a claimant to the Scottish throne. In 1332, backed by the English 'Disinherited' nobles, he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, won the Battle of Dupplin Moor and was crowned at Scone, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-war-of-scottish-independence">Second War of Scottish Independence</a>. Dependent on Edward III, he never won lasting support, and in 1356 he surrendered his claim to the English king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-balliol.jpg" alt="Edward Balliol" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uncertain since the image is not credited. The author of the book itself died in 1924. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Balliol,_King_of_Scotland_seal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward Balliol was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-balliol">John Balliol</a> and Isabella de Warenne. In 1295, when his father negotiated an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Edward was betrothed to Joan of Valois, eldest daughter of Charles, Count of Valois, but the betrothal was cancelled when John resigned the Scottish throne in 1296. After his father's abdication Edward was a prisoner in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> until 1299, when he was released into the custody of his grandfather John de Warenne, 6th Earl of Surrey. He possibly married Margaret of Taranto around 1331; if so, the marriage was childless and had been annulled by 1332.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the death of Robert I in June 1329 left the six-year-old David II as king, Balliol was recruited by the disinherited, men whose Scottish lands and titles Robert had confiscated after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bannockburn">Battle of Bannockburn</a>. With covert support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>, they invaded in 1332, and Balliol's force defeated the regent, the Earl of Mar, at the Battle of Dupplin Moor on 11 August 1332. He was crowned at Scone on 24 September, but three months later a surprise attack by nobles loyal to David II at the Battle of Annan drove him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>In 1333 Edward III openly declared for Balliol, and they returned with an English army, captured Berwick and defeated the Scots at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halidon-hill">Battle of Halidon Hill</a>. Under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> in 1334 Balliol ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a> to Edward and paid homage to him. His position stayed insecure because of quarrels among his supporters and fighting by pro-Bruce forces. A pro-Balliol army under Strathbogie was defeated at the Battle of Culblean on 30 November 1335, which was the effective end of his attempt, and by 1338 the tide had turned against him. After David II's defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nevilles-cross">Battle of Neville's Cross</a> in 1346 he raised an insurrection in Galloway, but held only part of it, and his power there diminished until 1355.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 20 January 1356 Balliol surrendered his claim to the Scottish throne to Edward III in exchange for an English pension of 2,000 pounds a year. He spent the rest of his life near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> and died childless in January 1364 at Wheatley, Doncaster. The location of his grave has been speculated to lie under a Doncaster post office.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Balliol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Near Perth, a Scottish force under Donald, Earl of Mar, possibly as many as 40,000 men, attacked about 1,500 largely English troops under <b>Edward Balliol</b> and was routed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Outside besieged Berwick, Edward III and <b>Edward Balliol</b> defeated a Scottish army under the regent Archibald Douglas that was trying to relieve the town.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The English-backed <b>Edward Balliol</b> then established his authority over most of Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Doncaster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.52,-1.13&amp;year=1335" aria-label="Doncaster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4525 324 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Edward Balliol"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4975 594h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4975 594h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.52,-1.13&amp;year=1335">What was here in 1335? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
